---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const sqlCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: 'SELECT name, price FROM products', note: 'The workhorse sentence: pick columns from a table. SQL reads like English on purpose — designed for managers in 1974, adopted by everyone since.' },
    { math: 'WHERE price > 100', note: 'Filter rows. The database scans and returns only matches — indexes (data structures behind the scenes) make this instant instead of a full scan.' },
    { math: 'JOIN customers ON id = customer_id', note: 'Combine tables by matching keys. Normalisation keeps each fact in one table; joins stitch them back into any view you need.' },
    { math: 'GROUP BY category — aggregates', note: 'Count, sum, average per group. This is where a database becomes an analysis engine, not just a filing cabinet.' },
    { math: 'ACID — the trust guarantee', note: 'Transactions are Atomic (all or nothing), Consistent, Isolated, Durable. When a bank transfer debits one account and credits another, ACID is why both happen or neither.' },
  ],
};

const sqlQuiz = {
  questions: [
    {
      q: "An orders table stores the customer's name and address on every order row. Which anomaly is this design GUARANTEED to produce eventually?",
      choices: [
        "Update anomaly — a customer changes address and old orders disagree with new ones",
        "The database runs slower on every SELECT",
        "Foreign keys stop working",
        "None — copying data is harmless",
      ],
      answer: 0,
      why: "Storing a fact in many rows (violating normalisation) means every copy is a chance to disagree: change the address and history silently splits. Store the address once in customers; JOIN when you need it.",
    },
    {
      q: "A transaction debits account A by 100 and credits account B by 100. The server loses power after the first write. Which ACID property prevents the 100 from vanishing?",
      choices: ["Isolation", "Atomicity (plus durability for the crash)", "Consistency", "Normalisation"],
      answer: 1,
      why: "Atomicity makes the two writes one unit — both happen or neither; the write-ahead log makes the outcome durable across the crash. Isolation is about concurrent transactions; consistency is about constraints holding.",
    },
    {
      q: "You add an index on the orders.status column, which takes 6 values and appears in every WHERE clause. What actually happens?",
      choices: [
        "All queries get faster",
        "Range queries on status speed up marginally, but the index wastes space and taxes every insert — low cardinality columns index poorly",
        "Writes get faster too, since lookups are faster",
        "The database automatically drops useless indexes",
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: "With 6 distinct values across millions of rows, each index entry still points at ~1/6 of the table — barely better than a scan, while every INSERT must now update the index too. Index selectivity (cardinality) is the real design variable.",
    },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/advanced/databases-and-sql">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Every serious application — banking, booking, medical records — sits on a <strong>relational
    database</strong>: data in typed tables, shaped by a schema, queried in SQL, and wrapped in guarantees
    spreadsheets cannot make (safe concurrent access, integrity by construction, durability after crashes).
    The design skill splits in two: <strong>schema design</strong> (how to store each fact exactly once, with
    enforced relationships) and <strong>querying</strong> (how to reassemble those facts into any view you
    need, fast). Codd's 1970 model and SQL from 1974 are still the substrate — making this among the most
    durable skills in computing.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the five SQL sentences that cover 90% of real work" widget="equationStep" config={sqlCfg} />

  <h2>Tables, types and keys: integrity by construction</h2>
  <p>
    A relational database stores data in <strong>relations</strong> (tables of rows, each row a tuple) whose
    columns have <strong>declared types</strong> — INTEGER, DECIMAL, TEXT, DATE, BOOLEAN. Types are not
    decoration: they constrain what can enter ("quantity = -5" or "price = 'banana'" is refused at the
    door), fix storage layout and comparison behaviour (DECIMAL for money, because binary floats cannot
    represent 0.1 exactly — a genuine bug class), and make indexes possible. A <strong>primary key</strong>
    uniquely identifies each row — either a natural key that exists in the world (ISBN, national ID) or a
    surrogate (an auto-incrementing id) chosen precisely because worldly identifiers change; the professional
    default is surrogate keys inside, natural unique constraints alongside. A <strong>foreign key</strong>
    declares that orders.customer_id must match some customers.id — and the database then <em>refuses</em>
    to insert an order for a nonexistent customer or delete a customer who still has orders. This is the
    syllabus's single-table topic generalised correctly: even a one-table database benefits from typed
    columns and a declared key; multi-table systems add enforced references.
  </p>

  <h2>Normalisation: the anomaly that justifies the theory</h2>
  <p>
    The one-table spreadsheet looks simpler — orders with columns order_id, customer_name, customer_address,
    product, qty — until the <strong>anomalies</strong> bite. <strong>Update anomaly</strong>: a customer
    moves house; you change the address on order 4,417 but miss 4,301 — the table now disagrees with itself.
    <strong>Insert anomaly</strong>: a new customer with no orders cannot exist in a table keyed by order.
    <strong>Delete anomaly</strong>: cancel their only order and the customer vanishes from your records.
    The cure is <strong>normalisation</strong>: one fact, one place. Split into customers(id, name,
    address), products(id, name, price), orders(id, customer_id, order_date), order_items(order_id,
    product_id, qty) — first normal form (atomic values, no repeating groups), then the deeper forms
    (2NF, 3NF) remove facts that depend on only part of a key or on other non-key columns; the practical
    target is 3NF. The cost is paid at query time: the "who bought what" view now requires JOINs. The
    mature position: normalise by default, denormalise <em>deliberately</em> for measured performance,
    accepting the staleness risk with eyes open.
  </p>

  <h2>SQL and joins: reassembling the view</h2>
  <p>
    SQL is declarative — you state <em>what</em> you want, the engine decides <em>how</em>. Worked query on
    the schema above: which customers spent more than 500 this year?
  </p>
  <p>
    SELECT c.name, SUM(oi.qty * p.price) AS total FROM orders o JOIN order_items oi ON oi.order_id = o.id
    JOIN products p ON p.id = oi.product_id JOIN customers c ON c.id = o.customer_id WHERE o.order_date
    >= '2026-01-01' GROUP BY c.name HAVING SUM(oi.qty * p.price) > 500 ORDER BY total DESC;
  </p>
  <p>
    Read the machinery inside it. <strong>INNER JOIN</strong> matches rows on the keys and drops
    non-matches — customers with no orders this year disappear, which may be exactly wrong for the question
    ("who did <em>not</em> buy?" needs a LEFT JOIN, which keeps all left rows and fills missing right rows
    with NULL — the single most useful join distinction). <strong>GROUP BY</strong> collapses matched rows
    into buckets and aggregates (SUM, COUNT, AVG) per bucket; <strong>HAVING</strong> filters the buckets
    after aggregation (WHERE filters rows before). Watch the NULL semantics: "status <> 'shipped'" silently
    drops rows whose status is NULL, because NULL compares unknown — a bug class every professional has
    met. One declarative sentence; the engine chooses scan or index, join order and hash versus nested loops.
  </p>

  <h2>Indexes: trading writes for reads</h2>
  <p>
    An index is a B-tree built over a column (the data-structures lesson's disk tree, applied): lookups and
    range scans on indexed columns drop from a full table scan (O(n) rows read) to O(log n) node touches —
    a 10-million-row lookup in 3–4 block reads instead of millions. The tax: every INSERT and UPDATE must
    also update every index, and each index consumes storage. The design variable is <strong>selectivity</strong>
    (cardinality): an index on customer_id (highly varied) is gold; one on a 6-value status column barely
    beats a scan while still taxing every write. Index the columns you filter, join and sort on; measure
    with the planner (EXPLAIN), not folklore.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "add an index and the query gets faster." Sometimes it gets slower (the
  planner may still scan if the index is unselective), and writes always get slower. Indexes are a
  read/write arbitrage, priced per insert.</p>

  <h2>Transactions: the ACID guarantee</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>transaction</strong> groups statements into one all-or-nothing unit, with four properties.
    <strong>Atomicity</strong>: the transfer debits account A by 100 and credits B by 100 — if the server
    dies after the first write, the write-ahead log rolls the half-transaction back; money never evaporates.
    <strong>Consistency</strong>: constraints (foreign keys, checks) hold before and after — no transaction
    leaves a negative balance if the schema forbids one. <strong>Isolation</strong>: concurrent
    transactions do not see each other's intermediate states — the classic anomalies it prevents are the
    <em>dirty read</em> (reading another transaction's uncommitted data that may be rolled back) and the
    <em>lost update</em> (two transactions read the same balance, both add 50, both write — one increment
    vanishes). <strong>Durability</strong>: once committed, the transaction survives crashes — it is in the
    log, not just in RAM. Isolation is a spectrum, not a binary: SQL defines levels from READ UNCOMMITTED
    to SERIALIZABLE, and production systems commonly use READ COMMITTED or REPEATABLE READ, trading
    anomaly risk for concurrency; modern engines deliver it with locking or MVCC (multi-version concurrency
    control — readers never block writers), which is why one busy PostgreSQL database can serve thousands
    of simultaneous sessions honestly.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked migration: from spreadsheet to schema</h2>
  <p>
    The normalisation theory becomes concrete on a real artefact. Take a school register spreadsheet:
    one row per enrolment, columns student_name, student_dob, tutor_name, tutor_room, course_title,
    grade. The anomalies are already visible (the tutor's room changes and 400 rows need editing; a
    student with no courses cannot exist; deleting the last enrolment erases the student). The migration,
    step by step: <strong>identify entities</strong> (students, tutors, courses, enrolments — the nouns
    with their own identity); <strong>assign keys</strong> (surrogate ids for each); <strong>move
    attributes to the entity they describe</strong> (dob belongs to students; room to tutors); and
    <strong>model the relationship as its own table</strong> — enrolments(student_id, course_id, grade),
    because a student's relationship to a course is itself a fact with attributes. The result is four
    tables joined by foreign keys, update-proof by construction: the tutor's room exists once; history
    (a grade belongs to a specific enrolment, not to a name) is preserved; and the "who takes what"
    view is one join away. The migration is the syllabus's single-table topic graduate: the same data,
    reorganised around facts rather than rows-of-convenience.
  </p>

  <h2>Writing data: constraints, upserts and windows</h2>
  <p>
    Reads are half the language. <strong>INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE</strong> run inside the transaction
    machinery, and constraints turn business rules into database-enforced facts: UNIQUE prevents double
    enrolment, CHECK (grade IN ('A','B','C','D','F')) refuses typos, NOT NULL documents what is truly
    required. Two power tools separate intermediate from advanced SQL. <strong>Upserts</strong>
    (INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE in PostgreSQL) express "create or refresh" as one atomic
    statement — no read-then-write race. <strong>Window functions</strong> aggregate <em>without
    collapsing rows</em>: SELECT name, grade, RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY course_id ORDER BY grade DESC)
    gives every student their rank within their course while keeping all rows visible — impossible with
    plain GROUP BY, and the workhorse of analytics SQL (running totals, month-over-month deltas,
    top-N-per-group). The design habit to carry: when a query becomes a nested subquery pile, it is
    usually asking for a join restructure or a window function — the language rewards reformulation
    over nesting.
  </p>

  <h2>Designing the query: a planner's-eye pass</h2>
  <p>
    Since the planner chooses <em>how</em>, learn to predict its choices. For the customer-spending query
    above with 10 million orders, the planner's decision sequence: filter early (apply the date predicate
    first, if an index on order_date exists — cutting rows before the expensive joins); pick join
    algorithms (a <strong>hash join</strong> builds an in-memory table of the smaller side and probes
    with the larger — good for big unsorted inputs; a <strong>nested loop</strong> with an index on the
    inner side wins when the outer side is small, as after a selective filter); choose join
    <em>order</em> to keep intermediate row counts small (joining customers to the already-filtered
    orders, not the reverse). Compare with the naive execution — join everything, filter at the end —
    which touches every row of every table. The habit that follows: write SQL by stating the
    <em>logical</em> question, then read EXPLAIN to see the physical plan, and fix data problems (missing
    indexes, stale statistics, functions wrapped around indexed columns — which disable index use)
    rather than rewriting the query into ever-cleverer forms. The database is usually better at
    restructuring than you are; your job is to give it something well-modelled and well-measured.
  </p>

  <h2>Data types, worked: why the choice bites</h2>
  <p>
    The syllabus's "data types" topic deserves its consequences spelled out. <strong>Money in
    DECIMAL</strong>: binary floats represent 0.1 approximately (0.1 + 0.2 = 0.30000000000000004), so
    pricing arithmetic in floats accumulates errors that audits find and courts punish; DECIMAL stores
    exact base-10 digits. <strong>Dates as DATE, not strings</strong>: '2026-03-05' sorts correctly as
    text only by ISO luck; a typed DATE gives range queries, indexing and arithmetic ("30 days ago") for
    free and rejects '5 March' at the door. <strong>Booleans and enums</strong>: a status column typed
    as an enumerated set documents the state machine in the schema itself and makes invalid states
    unrepresentable. <strong>Fixed vs variable text</strong> (CHAR vs VARCHAR) trades padding for
    flexibility — a two-letter country code wants CHAR(2). The meta-principle: <strong>types are the
    cheapest constraints you will ever buy</strong> — enforced at every insert by every client, needing
    no application code, surviving the next developer who has not read your comments. Schema is policy;
    a well-typed table is an application that partly enforces its own correctness.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Inside the query planner.</strong> A database does not run your SQL; it optimises it. The
    planner estimates selectivities from statistics, enumerates join orders (for 8 tables, 17,000 orderings
    — heuristics required), and picks scan and join algorithms from cost models; reading EXPLAIN output is
    the professional's debugging skill, and a "fast query" is really "a plan the planner could estimate
    well" (stale statistics are the classic cause of sudden slowness). <strong>Beyond one machine</strong>:
    the CAP theorem proves a distributed database cannot simultaneously guarantee consistency and
    availability under network partition — so the NoSQL landscape is a choice space: document stores
    (MongoDB) trade joins for flexible schema; key-value stores (Redis) trade query richness for
    microsecond caches; wide-column stores (Cassandra) trade transactions for write scale; graph databases
    model networks natively. The professional heuristic stands: choose by access pattern, not fashion — and
    notice that "eventually consistent" is a product decision made visible to users, not a technicality.
    <strong>Open problems</strong>: HTAP systems serving analytics and transactions on one copy; learning-
    based query optimisers; and the long-running empirical question of whether any interface will displace
    SQL — fifty years of challengers have so far only enriched it.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Codd, "A Relational Model of Data for Large Shared Data Banks" (1970); Chamberlin &amp; Boyce (1974); PostgreSQL documentation; Silberschatz, Korth &amp; Sudarshan, <em>Database System Concepts</em>.</p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="check yourself: anomalies, ACID in a crash, and index selectivity" widget="quiz" config={sqlQuiz} />
</TopicPage>
